<% sweep = Lab::SWEEPS.fetch("mutation_rate_long") %>
<% short_sweep = Lab::SWEEPS.fetch("mutation_rate") %>
<% seeds = sweep.fetch(:seeds).size %>
<% planned = sweep.fetch(:param_grid).fetch("mutation_rate").size * seeds %>
<% epochs = number_with_delimiter sweep.fetch(:epochs), delimiter: " " %>
<% short_epochs = number_with_delimiter short_sweep.fetch(:epochs), delimiter: " " %>
<% terminal = findings_finished_count(experiment_slug: "mutation-rate-long") %>

<%# The recorded result, read on 2026-09-11. Runs are named by arm and seed, never by a
    database id, and the live tables further down re-read the same runs. %>
<% arms = [
     { exponent: 14, flagged: 1, confirmed: 0, transitions: [], detector_only: [[3, 39_320]] },
     { exponent: 13, flagged: 2, confirmed: 2, transitions: [[1, 5_030, 867], [5, 7_000, 108]],
       detector_only: [] },
     { exponent: 12, flagged: 4, confirmed: 3,
       transitions: [[9, 15_560, 57], [10, 21_540, 123], [2, 47_820, 105]], detector_only: [[5, 43_220]] },
     { exponent: 11, flagged: 1, confirmed: 1, transitions: [[10, 42_730, 36]], detector_only: [] }
   ] %>

<h2>Hypothesis</h2>
<p>
  The <%= link_to "mutation-rate sweep", finding_path("mutation-rate-window") %> stopped
  every run at <%= short_epochs %> epochs, and 95 of its 100 runs were still in a random
  soup when the clock ran out. A sweep censored that heavily measures its own budget as
  much as its parameter: an arm showing nothing may hold a hazard that is genuinely lower,
  or the same hazard watched for too short a time. The two readings separate on a longer
  run. If emergence at these rates is <em>rare</em> — a low hazard per epoch — three times
  the budget buys about three times the events and the arms keep their order. If the short
  sweep was instead watching a <em>slow</em> process, the extra epochs produce events out
  of arms that read as empty, and the window the first sweep failed to find could still be
  there, displaced past epoch <%= short_epochs %>.
</p>

<h2>Method</h2>
<p>
  The four rates that bracket the short sweep's transitions —
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-14</sup></span> to <span class="mono">2<sup>-11</sup></span>,
  the arm below the lowest transition through the arm above the highest of this
  neighbourhood — on the same 128×128 torus of 64-byte tapes, <%= seeds %> seeds each,
  <%= epochs %> epochs per run. Every parameter that enters the simulation is at the short
  sweep's value, and only the snapshot cadence is sparser — a snapshot is written output
  and not state — so a run here is the same run the short sweep made, byte for byte, up to
  epoch <%= short_epochs %>, and then keeps going: this is a re-run rather than a new
  sample, which is what makes the two sweeps directly comparable and also what stops the
  overlap from counting twice. The dependent variable is the transition epoch of DESIGN
  §1.2 — the first sampled epoch at which <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> falls
  below 0.6 and stays below for three further samples — and beside it the
  <span class="mono">replicator_count</span> census. A run is called census-confirmed here
  when both fire in it.
</p>

<h2>Result</h2>
<p>
  <%= terminal %> of the <%= planned %> runs have reached <%= epochs %> epochs. Every run
  still on the clock is already past its own transition epoch and past its own census peak,
  so no count below is waiting on them. The table is the recorded per-arm result; the phase
  diagram and the per-run rows below read the same runs live.
</p>

<div class="table-scroll">
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Mutation rate</th>
        <th class="numeric">Flagged</th>
        <th class="numeric">Census-confirmed</th>
        <th>Transition epochs (peak census)</th>
        <th>Flagged with an empty census</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <% arms.each do |arm| %>
        <% rate = 2.0**-arm.fetch(:exponent) %>
        <tr>
          <td class="mono">2<sup>-<%= arm.fetch(:exponent) %></sup></td>
          <td class="numeric mono"><%= arm.fetch(:flagged) %> of <%= seeds %></td>
          <td class="numeric mono"><%= arm.fetch(:confirmed) %> of <%= seeds %></td>
          <td class="mono">
            <% if arm.fetch(:transitions).empty? %>
              —
            <% else %>
              <%= safe_join(arm.fetch(:transitions).sort_by { |transition| transition[1] }
                               .map { |seed, epoch, peak|
                    safe_join([number_with_delimiter(epoch, delimiter: " "), " (",
                               findings_run_link(experiment_slug: "mutation-rate-long", seed: seed,
                                                 params: { "mutation_rate" => rate }),
                               ", #{peak})"])
                  }, ", ") %>
            <% end %>
          </td>
          <td class="mono">
            <% if arm.fetch(:detector_only).empty? %>
              —
            <% else %>
              <%= safe_join(arm.fetch(:detector_only).map { |seed, epoch|
                    safe_join([number_with_delimiter(epoch, delimiter: " "), " (",
                               findings_run_link(experiment_slug: "mutation-rate-long", seed: seed,
                                                 params: { "mutation_rate" => rate }),
                               ")"])
                  }, ", ") %>
            <% end %>
          </td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p class="text-sm text-muted">
  "Peak census" is the largest <span class="mono">replicator_count</span> the run reached
  at any sample. The last column is the runs the detector flagged whose census never left
  zero.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>The short sweep was right, and it under-counted.</strong> Within the same four
  arms the <%= short_epochs %>-epoch budget found three transitions; the
  <%= epochs %>-epoch budget finds eight, six of them census-confirmed — 6 of
  <%= planned %> runs, 15%. The six confirmed transitions land at 5 030, 7 000, 15 560,
  21 540, 42 730 and 47 820 epochs, a median of 18 550, and three of them fall after epoch
  <%= short_epochs %>: half of the emergence in this region of the parameter space happens
  where the first sweep had already stopped watching. That is the answer to the question
  the sweep was queued for. Emergence here is rare — three events in the first
  <%= short_epochs %> epochs of these runs and three more in the 40 000 that follow, which
  is what a roughly constant hazard per epoch looks like — rather than a process that was
  merely slow to start. Six events cannot tell a constant hazard from a gently falling one,
  but they do rule out the reading in which the short budget was missing a burst.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>A disagreement that was only the clock.</strong> The
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-12</sup></span> arm's ninth seed is the run three earlier
  sweeps have all recorded — it flags at epoch 15 560 under the mutation-rate, world-size
  and radius grids alike, every one of which stops at <%= short_epochs %>, and in all three
  its census is zero at every sample. Run for <%= epochs %> epochs, the same run counts its
  first replicating cell at epoch 26 140 and peaks at 57. The
  <%= link_to "first sweep's", finding_path("mutation-rate-window") %> flagged-with-no-census
  disagreement, in this run, was the budget and not the substrate: the detector saw the tape
  statistics turn over, and the census needed another 10 000 epochs to see the lineage that
  had done it.
</p>

<% cited_chart = findings_run_chart(experiment_slug: "mutation-rate-long", seed: 9, metric: "replicator_count",
                                    params: { "mutation_rate" => 2.0**-12 }) %>
<% if cited_chart %>
  <p>
    That run's census, with its transition epoch marked: flat at zero across the whole span
    the short sweep watched, and only then lifting off.
  </p>

  <%= cited_chart %>
<% end %>

<p>
  <strong>Determinism, checked once more.</strong> The two earliest transitions here — the
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-13</sup></span> arm's first and fifth seeds at 5 030 and 7 000
  epochs — are the short sweep's two earliest, at the same epochs, from the same
  <span class="mono">(params, seed)</span>. One of them peaks at 867 replicating cells, the
  largest census of either sweep. Nothing about the re-run is new evidence up to epoch
  <%= short_epochs %>; that is the point, and it is the guarantee DESIGN §1.1 makes,
  checked rather than asserted.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>The lower cutoff survives three times the budget.</strong> The
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-14</sup></span> arm has no census-confirmed run at
  <%= epochs %> epochs, as it had none at <%= short_epochs %>. Its one flagged run collapses
  <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> only to 0.900 by the last epoch, holds 16 370 of
  its 16 384 tapes distinct and never counts a replicating cell — the low-entropy attractor
  the other sweeps also report, not a lineage. The hint of a cutoff in the first sweep was
  the weakest thing it claimed, and the obvious way for it to be wrong was censoring; that
  way out is now closed for this arm. What the arm is not is a demonstrated zero: 10 seeds
  that produce nothing bound the hazard, they do not abolish it.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>Two runs flagged with an empty census, and one that did not hold.</strong> The
  detector fires twice on runs whose <span class="mono">replicator_count</span> never leaves
  zero — the <span class="mono">2<sup>-14</sup></span> arm's third seed at 39 320 and the
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-12</sup></span> arm's fifth seed at 43 220, 2 of
  <%= planned %> runs — and the second of those ends with
  <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> back at 0.951 and every tape distinct, which is
  the soup it started in. Read on the detector alone this sweep has 8 events; read on the
  census it has 6, and the two disagree in both directions across the programme. Separately,
  the <span class="mono">2<sup>-12</sup></span> arm's tenth seed shows that a confirmed
  emergence is not a terminal state: it transitions at 21 540, its census peaks at 123 cells
  by 28 100, and by epoch <%= epochs %> the world has climbed back to a
  <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> of 0.952 with all 16 384 tapes distinct. The
  lineage spread, saturated and dissolved, and the world it left behind is indistinguishable
  from one where nothing ever happened. A longer budget does not only add events; it also
  shows some of them ending.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>The window is still not there.</strong> Confirmed emergence lands 0, 2, 3 and 1 of
  <%= seeds %> from <span class="mono">2<sup>-14</sup></span> to
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-11</sup></span>. That is the same shape the first sweep reported
  at a third of the budget — a floor with nothing below it and no peak above it that
  <%= seeds %> seeds can resolve — and 3 of <%= seeds %> against 1 of <%= seeds %> is not a
  distinguishable pair of arms. The extra epochs sharpened the claim about <em>when</em>
  emergence happens without moving the claim about <em>where</em> in the parameter space it
  does.
</p>

<p>
  This stays partial rather than published for two reasons. Runs are still on the clock, so
  the denominator above is read live and the arms may still gain an event. And every arm is
  censored again at <%= epochs %> epochs: the latest confirmed transition of the sweep fell
  at 47 820, four fifths of the way through the budget, which is precisely the pattern that
  says the next budget would find more.
</p>

<h2>What next</h2>
<p>
  The programme's cheapest events are not here. This sweep spent 2.4 million run-epochs for
  six confirmed transitions; the
  <%= link_to "world-size sweep", finding_path("world-size-scaling") %> reads emergence as a
  hazard per cell-epoch, which makes a wider world the efficient way to buy events and a
  longer run the expensive one. The reading to test next is whether the two trade off the
  way that hazard predicts — 60 000 epochs at 128² and 20 000 at 256² are within a factor of
  three of the same cell-epoch budget and should produce comparable counts. The
  <%= link_to "BFF positive control", finding_path("bff-control") %> still gates reading any
  silent arm as a negative, the <span class="mono">2<sup>-14</sup></span> arm included. And
  the transient at <span class="mono">2<sup>-12</sup></span> is an argument for watching the
  census rather than the detector: a run that ends in a random soup can still have held a
  hundred replicating cells in the middle, and only the stored samples say so.
</p>
